        <briefdescription>
<para>Compares the operands with <computeroutput>&lt;=</computeroutput>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Always inlined, also at <computeroutput>-O0</computeroutput>, so that the debug builds do not get one more function for each combination of operand types.</para>
<para><parameterlist kind="templateparam"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>Lhs_T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The type of the left-hand operand. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>Rhs_T</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The type of the right-hand operand.</para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>lhs</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The left-hand operand. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>rhs</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The right-hand operand. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The result of <computeroutput>lhs &lt;= rhs</computeroutput>, converted to <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>.</para>
</simplesect>
The using-declaration makes the standard operators visible to unqualified lookup, in addition to those found by argument-dependent lookup, and hides any <computeroutput>operator&lt;=</computeroutput> of the enclosing namespaces. </para>
        </detaileddescription>
